  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... First snowfall for parts of the Alleghenies with \nspotty showers & brisk conditions elsewhere through Saturday.\n\nIncreased snow coverage and accums tonight across western\nHighland and western Pendleton given latest obs and hi-res\nguidance. Already coating of snow observed on highest cameras in\nthe Alleghenies. Did issue an SPS through daybreak for these\nareas as they could see an inch or two on the highest ridges.\nPrevious discussion follows...\n\nAs the low pushes east and northwesterly flow increase expect \nthe coverage of showers eventually changing to snow showers over\nthe Alleghenies to increase heading into Friday and Friday \nnight.\n\n00, 06, and 12z guidance has backed off quite a bit in regards to \nsnow amounts with the bulk of the accumulations stretched over a 24 \nto 30 hour period. Marginal temperatures at onset will also impact \naccumulations with most of the snow falling Friday morning into \nFriday afternoon. Guidance has come into better agreement in lagging \nsnow shower chances over the mountains through at least Saturday \nbefore things begin to shutdown as high pressure builds back into \nthe region. Friday night into Saturday looks to be the most \nimpactful snow at least when it comes to untreated surfaces given \ntemperatures below freezing and snow ratios on the order of 12:1 \nincreasing to 14-16:1 at times. These will of course be maximized at \nelevations above 2000 feet and in particularly the upslope favored \nzones of western Garrett, western Pendleton, and western Grant \ncounties. If travel disruptions were to materializes, expect impacts \nto US-219 from the MD/PA line to WV line, US-48 from Mount Storm, WV \nwest to Davis, WV, and US-33 west of Franklin, WV. Snow \naccumulations will remain on the order of a coating to 2\" with \nthe greater probabilities of 1-2\" with localized 3\" amounts \nabove 3000 feet pending any squalls/streamers (mainly late\nFriday night through Saturday midday).\n\nTemperatures will begin to drop tonight as this frontal passage \nmoves through, continuing to get chillier into the weekend as winds \ngust up to 25 knots Friday-Sunday. Additional rain showers are \nlikely primarily north & east of the I-70 corridor throughout the \nday Friday. Most rain east of the Blue Ridge likely diminishes by \nFriday evening, with temperatures becoming noticeably colder between \nFriday and Sunday. Overnight lows range in the upper 20s to mid 30s \nareawide through the weekend, with daytime temperatures generally in \nthe 40s to low 50s & remaining near freezing across the\nmountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures and dry condtions expected into \nthe middle of next week.\n\nUpper level troughing remains over the east coast through early next \nweek with upper level ridging pivoting overhead by midweek. At the \nsurface, high pressure builds in from the west yielding dry \nconditions. Conditions dry out areawide on Sunday as winds weaken in \nthe afternoon and upslope snow becomes less likely. As high pressure \nbuilds overhead, light winds can be expected through next week along \nwith dry conditions areawide. As far as temperatures, below normal \ntemperatures continue early in the week with high temperatures \nranging from the 30s in the Alleghenies to the mid 50s in the metros \non Sunday. As high pressure settles overhead, temperatures moderate \nwith highs in the 50s and 60s by Wednesday. Overnight low \ntemperatures drop into the 20s to low 30s for most with only those \nin the metros staying in the mid 30s. The growing season ends this \nweekend so frost/freeze headlines will not be issued.",
